// Tables of text, laid out so the columns line up -- a package, in the sense `design/packages.md`
// gives the word.
//
// There is no `dependencies` block because this depends on nothing sysl could fetch: it is one file
// of sysl over the standard module's buffers and its Unicode width data, and the standard module is
// not a package.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what arrived
// against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       table { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/table", version = "0.1.3" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.1.3` on this repository. The coordinate names the *package*, `table`, while the module a
// program imports is `sh.sysl.table`; `packages.md § 9` keeps those two deliberately apart.

package {
  name    = "table"
  version = "0.1.3"
}

// **`alloc`, because a table is a growable thing and cannot be anything else.** How many rows there
// will be is the caller's business and usually not known until the last one has been added, and each
// cell holds the bytes its value rendered to -- so the storage is a `Buf` of cells and a `Buf` of
// bytes per cell, both of which are the heap.
//
// This is the difference between this package and `harness`, which is also pure sysl and declares
// nothing: a framework for testing on a board may not ask the board for an allocator, so its state is
// a fixed number of bytes laid into the image. A table's is not fixed, so it asks.
//
// **What is NOT here is `os` or `posix`.** Nothing in this package opens, reads or writes anything:
// `render` takes the sink it is to write through, and where that sink goes is the caller's decision.
// A table therefore renders on a target with no operating system, provided the target has somewhere
// to put the bytes.
requires {
  alloc = true
}
